.\" Title: vrunner-cert-generate
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-cert-generate.1

.TH VRUNNER\-CERT\-GENERATE 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-cert\-generate \- generate a new named certificate for the certificate pool

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBcert generate\fR \fINAME\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
Generate a new self-signed X.509 certificate and add it to the vrunner certificate pool. The certificate is generated with a 2048-bit RSA key pair, valid for 365 days. Both the certificate and private key are saved to \fB~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/\fR, and the pool entry is registered in the vrunner configuration file (global or local).

Each certificate has a derived bearer token computed as the SHA-256 hash of the certificate PEM content, hex-encoded as a 64-character string. This token is used for application-level authorization: when a command is bound to a certificate, API requests to that command must present the matching bearer token in the \fBAuthorization\fR header.

The certificate pool enables per-command access control in multi-tenant environments. For example, a CI pipeline can have its own certificate, allowing it to access only commands bound to that certificate. Similarly, different teams or services can each have their own certificates with independent tokens.

.SH ARGUMENTS

.TP
.B NAME
A unique identifier for the certificate (e.g., \fBmy-app\fR, \fBteam-alpha\fR, \fBci-pipeline\fR). Used to reference the certificate when binding it to commands and when using \fBcert show\fR, \fBcert remove\fR, or the API. The name must be unique within the pool; generating a certificate with a name that already exists will overwrite the previous entry.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Generate a certificate:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert generate my-app\fR
Certificate 'my-app' generated successfully.
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/key.pem
  Token:      a3f8c1e2b7d94001... (first 16 of 64 chars)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Generate a certificate for a deployment pipeline:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner cert generate deploy-$(date +%Y%m%d)\fR
Certificate 'deploy-20250527' generated successfully.
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/deploy-20250527/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/deploy-20250527/key.pem
  Token:      7c1e9d4a8f2b3c01... (first 16 of 64 chars)
.fi
.RE

.PP
Use the generated certificate with a spawned command:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ TOKEN=$(vrunner cert show my-app | grep "Token:" | awk '{print $2}')\fR
\fB$ curl -s -X POST http://localhost:9090/api/commands \e\fR
     \fB-H "Content-Type: application/json" \e\fR
     \fB-d '{"cmd": "node", "args": ["server.js"], "certificate": "my-app"}'\fR
.fi
.RE

.SH FILES

.TP
.B ~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/cert.pem
Generated certificate file.

.TP
.B ~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/key.pem
Generated private key file.

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Certificate generated and registered successfully.

.TP
.B 1
Error (certificate generation failed, configuration write failed, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

